In natural language processing, a commonly employed method for breaking down
continuous text into individual terms is essential for various purposes, such as
searching and data retrieval. Although the N-gram model and hidden Markov models
have been useful tools for this task in Western languages, Japanese term segmentation
has presented a distinct challenge due to the complex layout of written text. A
range of custom methods has been developed to address this issue, including those
leveraging context-free grammars, neural networks, and bi-directional LSTMs. Building
on previous work, we introduce an enhanced variant of the LSTM architecture, which
has yielded exceptional accuracy in Japanese term segmentation, surpassing even
specialized methods in various evaluations. We also highlight the importance of word
order in Japanese and explore the enhanced characteristics of our proposed model. 
Furthermore, we demonstrate the effectiveness of our model in integrating domain-specific
knowledge through pre-training and showcase its ability to automatically generate
domain-specific term segmentations with high precision. All relevant code and data
have been made publicly accessible. 

\textit{In this study, we aim to achieve state-of-the-art results for Japanese term}
\textit{segmentation using standard characters without any supplementary data.} 
\textit{Building on prior research, we enhance the model's performance, surpassing}
\textit{specialized methods in various evaluations. We also examine the underlying}
\textit{characteristics of our proposed model and its potential for integrating domain-}
\textit{specific knowledge through pre-training.} 

\textit{All relevant code and data have been made publicly accessible at}
