This paper explores a class of gauge theories, $SO(N_{c})$, and investigates how the center of the gauge group affects the theory's properties when matter fields with different representations are added to the system. We examine how this effect plays out in various phases of the theory as well as in different stable phases, and our analysis reveals how the $SO(N_{c})$ theories differ from $SU(N_{c})$ theories, particularly in how charges are organized. We then discuss strategies for directly showing the theories' confinement properties and how the gauge group's center is exposed in a physical sense. Since such a manifestation may not be straightforward, we also identify a limit in which the theories become similar to $SU(N_{c})$ gauge theories in a different representation, and in this limit, our analysis provides a clear explanation of the theories' confinement and the breaking of chiral symmetry. Finally, we explore a phase where the theories act like free theories due to the matter fields' behavior, and in this phase, the theories' properties are closely tied to the theories with a fully broken chiral symmetry. In summary, our study delves into the unique characteristics of $SO(N_{c})$ gauge theories, particularly in how the center of the gauge group is linked to the theory's properties in different phases and in different representations of the matter fields. 

\textit{Note: The paper focuses particularly on $SO(N_{c} = 3)$ theories, though its conclusions and analyses can be extended to $SO(N_{c} = 5, 7)$ theories as well.} 

