To better understand chaotic systems, a unique statistical approach was previously developed for the three-body problem, using transport theory to predict the rate at which orbits leave a region of phase space. This statistical model was successfully applied to the late stages of the three-body problem at high energies, where the rates of escape and close encounters with a distance of less than 0.0075 without escape were accurately calculated. We have now developed a method to predict the likelihood of close encounters based on initial conditions, and have tested this approach for the first time in a large set of N=100,000 initial conditions within a region of phase space. The results show a strong correlation with escape and the accuracy of our predictions, demonstrating the potential for our statistical model to help forecast mayfly outcomes in the neighborhood of the late stage three-body problem. The prediction tool is available online. 

